Status: accepted on 2026-09-22, migration in progress. Authors: Dana Whitfield (platform) and Kofi Mensah (security). Reviewers: the architecture group. Last revised 2026-09-30.

## Summary

This RFC sets out how Querent keeps each customer's data separate from every other customer's data, in the database, the search cluster, object storage, caches, background jobs and exports. It replaces application-only filtering with Postgres row-level security on every tenant table, adds per-tenant encryption keys for survey responses, and keeps separate databases only for customers on the Dedicated plan. It also describes the migration, how we will test isolation, and what is still open.

The short version for most engineers: every query that touches tenant data runs inside a transaction that has the tenant set, the database refuses rows from any other tenant, and there is no way around that in normal service code.

## Background and history {agent=skip}

Querent started in 2019 as a survey tool for small research agencies. The first version ran as one Rails app on one Postgres database, and a "tenant" was simply an account row. Every table that held customer data had an `account_id` column, and every query was expected to filter on it. There were 40 customers at the end of the first year and the whole team could review every query that went in.

In 2021 the product moved to a service architecture. The survey builder, response collection, reporting and exports became separate services, mostly in Go, still sharing the one database. `account_id` was renamed to `tenant_id` in the same year, a migration that took four months and that people still mention when anyone proposes a schema change. At the same time we introduced the TenantScope library, a small package that wraps the database handle and adds the tenant filter to queries built through it.

ADR-0007, written in March 2024 by the platform team of the time, made TenantScope the official isolation mechanism and explicitly chose not to use database-level controls. The reasoning then was sound: row-level security was new to the team, there were worries about query plans, and the connection pooler we used could not carry session state reliably. ADR-0007 is quoted in the Current state section because its rules are still what the code follows today.

In November 2025 we had incident INC-311. A customer success manager ran a scheduled export for one tenant and received a file that also held 212 responses from a second tenant. The cause was a reporting query written with raw SQL that did not go through TenantScope, added to the export worker eight months earlier. Code review missed it because the query was in a long file and the filter looked present at a glance; it filtered on `survey.tenant_id` but joined responses by survey id from a cache that was keyed without the tenant. The second tenant was told within 48 hours. No other exports were affected, which we confirmed by replaying every export from the previous 90 days.

INC-311 was not the first near miss. In 2023 a support script that recalculated completion rates ran for every tenant with a missing filter and wrote one tenant's rates into 40 other tenants' dashboards for about an hour. Nobody outside the company saw response data, so it was logged as a bug and not as a security incident. In hindsight it was the same failure, and several people at the INC-311 review said so.

After INC-311 two things changed. The security team asked for isolation that does not depend on every engineer remembering a library call, and three enterprise prospects asked in the same quarter for evidence of database-level isolation during their security reviews. One of them, a public health research body, required a separate database outright. That is how the Dedicated plan came about, and it is the reason this RFC exists.

The Dedicated plan was launched in March 2026 as a sales exception before it had a design. The first Dedicated customer got a copy of the shared schema on its own instance, set up by hand by the infrastructure team over two weeks. The second and third were set up with scripts written for the first. Part of the work in this RFC is to make Dedicated databases a normal, supported part of the platform rather than three special cases that only two people know how to run.

The first draft of this RFC (revision 1, May 2026) proposed one Postgres schema per tenant. Revision 2 kept schemas but only for large tenants. Revision 3, discussed at the architecture review on 2026-07-14, dropped schemas in favour of row-level security for everyone. The revision history at the end of this document lists what changed in each revision. People who joined after July sometimes find old links to revision 1 in tickets, and those links describe a design we are not building.

## Terms

- **Tenant**: a paying customer organisation. One tenant has one row in `tenants` and a `tenant_id` (a UUID).
- **Workspace**: a team inside a tenant. A tenant has 1 to 200 workspaces. Isolation in this RFC is between tenants, not between workspaces. Workspace permissions are handled by the access-control service and are out of scope.
- **Tenant table**: any table with a `tenant_id` column. At the time of writing there are 87.
- **Global table**: a table without `tenant_id`, such as `plans`, `feature_flags` or `countries`.
- **Respondent**: a person answering a survey. Respondents are not Querent users and have no login.
- **Response data**: the answers respondents give, including free text and uploaded files. It is the most sensitive data we hold.
- **Dedicated plan**: the enterprise plan where a tenant gets its own database instance.
- **Break-glass access**: time-limited access to one tenant's data by a Querent engineer, granted through a ticket.

## Current state

Querent runs in two regions, EU and US. Each region has the same set of services and its own primary database. Tenants are pinned to one region when they sign up and never move between regions without a migration project.

As of 2026-09-01, the two regions together hold:

- 1,840 tenants, of which 61 are on the Enterprise plan and 3 are on the Dedicated plan;
- about 410 million survey responses, 3.2 TB in the primary databases;
- 14 TB of uploaded files in object storage;
- a largest tenant of 38 million responses, and a median tenant of 41,000.

Seven services read or write tenant data: `builder`, `collect`, `reports`, `exports`, `notify`, `admin-api` and `billing-sync`. All of them use the shared primary database in their region, through a connection pooler in transaction mode. Background work runs on a job queue with 24 worker processes per region. Search for reports runs on a separate search cluster with one index per region. Analytics data is copied every night into a warehouse that the data team uses for product metrics and that some Enterprise customers query through the reporting API.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  accTitle: Current data flow for tenant data
  subgraph Services
    B[builder]
    C[collect]
    R[reports]
    E[exports]
    A[admin-api]
  end
  B --> P[(Primary DB)]
  C --> P
  R --> P
  R --> S[(Search cluster)]
  E --> P
  E --> O[(Object storage)]
  A --> P
  C --> Q[Job queue]
  Q --> W[Workers]
  W --> P
  W --> S
  P -- nightly copy --> D[(Warehouse)]
```

The rules that service code follows today come from ADR-0007. Its decision text reads:

:::decision{status=superseded}
Tenant isolation is enforced in the application layer. Every query on a tenant table must be built through TenantScope, which adds `tenant_id = $1` from the request context. Services must not use Postgres row-level security policies, because they make query plans harder to reason about and they do not work with our connection pooler in transaction mode. Code review is the control that catches queries written outside TenantScope, and each service team owns the review of its own queries.
:::

In practice, TenantScope covers about 92% of queries by count, measured by a log sample in June 2026. The rest are raw SQL in reports and exports, migrations, and admin tooling. A linter added after INC-311 flags raw SQL that mentions a tenant table, but it can be silenced with a comment, and 140 places in the code base carry that comment today.

## Goals and non-goals

Goals:

1. A query that reaches the database without a tenant set must return no tenant rows, rather than all of them.
2. A bug in one service must not be able to return or change another tenant's rows, in the primary database, search, caches, object storage or exports.
3. Customers on the Enterprise and Dedicated plans get isolation they can verify in a security review, with a written description and test evidence.
4. The change must not cost more than 5% in p95 latency on the main read paths (see Performance).
5. The migration must not need downtime for any tenant.

Non-goals:

- Isolation between workspaces inside one tenant is not part of this RFC. It stays with the access-control service.
- We are not moving tenants between regions, and we are not adding new regions.
- We are not changing how respondents are identified. Respondent data belongs to the tenant whose survey collected it, even when the same person answers surveys for two tenants.
- Encrypting every column with a per-tenant key is not a goal. Only response data gets per-tenant keys (see Encryption keys).
- We are not replacing the connection pooler.

## Requirements

Every tenant table must have a row-level security policy before the migration is called complete, and the policy must use the `app.tenant_id` setting. A tenant table without a policy fails the schema check in CI from phase 3 onward.

Service database roles must not have the `BYPASSRLS` attribute, and they must not own the tables they read. Table owners are migration roles that services never use at runtime.

The tenant is set once per transaction, at the start, from the authenticated request or the job payload. A transaction never changes its tenant. Code that needs to work across tenants opens one transaction per tenant.

Global tables stay without policies. A global table must never hold response data or anything that can identify a respondent. If a table needs both, it is split.

Any cache entry that holds tenant data has the tenant id in its key, at the start of the key. Cache entries for response data live at most 10 minutes.

:::danger
> **Warning:** Do not grant any runtime role the right to set `app.tenant_id` outside the `with_tenant` function described in Detailed design. A role that can set it freely can read every tenant's data, which is exactly the failure INC-311 showed us.
:::

Tenant data stays in the tenant's home region. No copy of a tenant's rows, files, search entries or cache entries may be written to the other region, including for backups, debugging or load tests. Backups are stored in a second location inside the same region.

Exports, reports and search results for one tenant are produced only from that tenant's data, with no cross-tenant joins. Cross-tenant reports exist only for Querent's own billing and product metrics, and they run in the warehouse against pseudonymised data, never in the primary database.

Response data at rest is encrypted with a key that belongs to the tenant. Other tenant data is encrypted at rest with the regional key, as today.

Isolation tests (see Testing) run on every merge to main for all seven services, and a failing isolation test blocks the deploy. There is no override for that block, except during a declared incident when the incident commander can allow a deploy that fixes the incident.

## Threat model

The threats this RFC addresses are the ones that come from our own code and our own people, not from outside attackers. Perimeter security, authentication and the gateway are covered by the security team's existing controls and are not changed here.

We considered these failure modes, roughly in order of how likely we think they are:

1. **A query without the tenant filter.** A developer writes raw SQL, or a new service forgets TenantScope, and a query returns or changes rows of every tenant. This is the INC-311 class of bug and the main reason for this RFC.
2. **A query with the wrong tenant filter.** The query filters, but on the wrong column or with a tenant id that came from the wrong place, such as a URL parameter or a cached object. INC-311 was partly this as well.
3. **A cache or index that mixes tenants.** Data is filtered correctly in the database but stored in a cache, search index or file under a key that another tenant's request can reach.
4. **A job that runs for the wrong tenant.** A job is enqueued with one tenant and run with another, for example after a retry that rebuilds the payload.
5. **Staff access beyond what is needed.** A support engineer or developer reads response data they did not need, through admin tools or direct database access.
6. **A stolen backup or database snapshot.** Someone outside the company gets hold of a backup file.

Row-level security addresses 1 and most of 2 directly. The cache client, the search aliases and the storage prefixes address 3. Job payload rules address 4. Break-glass access and the `admin-api` limits address 5. Per-tenant encryption of response data addresses 6, and also limits the damage of 1 to 5, since leaked answer values are unreadable without the tenant's key.

What this design does not protect against: a bug inside `with_tenant` itself, a compromised database superuser, or a compromised key management account. Those are covered by access controls on the platform team's own accounts and by the key management service's audit trail. We also do not protect against a tenant's own admin sharing their data, which is their choice.

An attacker who controls a valid session for tenant A cannot reach tenant B's data through the API under this design, as long as the gateway checks the session correctly. The fuzzing job in Testing exists to keep checking that.

## Options considered

We looked at five options. Each is described as it was presented at the architecture review on 2026-07-14, with the outcome recorded after it. The comparison table at the end of the section summarises them.

### Option A: keep application-level filtering, add more checks

:::decision{status=rejected date=2026-07-14}
Keep ADR-0007 as it is and make it harder to get wrong. TenantScope would become the only way to get a database handle, the linter would lose its silence comment, and a proxy in front of the database would log any query on a tenant table without a `tenant_id` predicate.

This is the cheapest option. It needs no schema change and no migration, and the platform team estimated it at 6 engineer-weeks. It also has a real weakness that the reviews kept coming back to. The control still sits in the application, so a raw query, a new service that does not use TenantScope, or a predicate that filters on the wrong column still returns other tenants' rows. The proxy only logs, it cannot tell a correct predicate from a wrong one, and INC-311 had a predicate that looked right. Two of the enterprise prospects said directly that application filtering would not pass their review.

Outcome: rejected at the 2026-07-14 review. The parts that are cheap and useful, removing the linter's silence comment and requiring TenantScope for new code, are kept as part of phase 1 of the migration.
:::

### Option B: shared tables with row-level security

:::decision{status=accepted}
Keep the shared tables and add a Postgres row-level security policy on every tenant table. Each transaction sets `app.tenant_id` at its start, and the policy only lets the transaction see and write rows where `tenant_id` matches. A query without the setting sees nothing. TenantScope stays as a convenience and as a second check, but it is no longer the control.

Row-level security works with the pooler in transaction mode as long as the setting is made with `SET LOCAL` inside the transaction, so it ends with the transaction and never leaks to the next client on the same connection. The concern in ADR-0007 came from the pooler's session mode, which we stopped using in 2025.

The costs are the migration itself, roughly 30 engineer-weeks across all teams, some query plan risk on the largest tables, and the discipline of setting the tenant in every transaction, including background jobs. The query plan risk was tested in a benchmark in June 2026, described in Performance.

Outcome: accepted, with per-tenant encryption keys for response data and the Dedicated plan as the exception. See Decision.
:::

### Option C: one schema per tenant

:::decision{status=rejected date=2026-07-14}
Give each tenant its own Postgres schema with a full copy of the tenant tables, and set the `search_path` per transaction. This was the proposal in revision 1 of this RFC.

Schemas give a clean boundary that customers understand, and dropping a tenant is a single statement. The problems are scale and operations. With 1,840 tenants and 87 tenant tables we would have about 160,000 tables per region, growing with every new tenant. Every migration would run 1,840 times. Our migration tool takes about 4 seconds per schema for a typical change, so a single migration would take over two hours per region, and a failure halfway leaves tenants on different schema versions. Planner statistics, vacuum and monitoring all get harder with that many tables. We also found that 70% of tenants have fewer than 5,000 responses, so most schemas would be nearly empty.

Revision 2 tried to limit schemas to the 61 Enterprise tenants. That kept the operational cost lower but gave us two isolation models to maintain, test and explain, and it still left the smaller tenants on application filtering.

Outcome: rejected at the 2026-07-14 review, for both the all-tenants and the Enterprise-only variants.
:::

### Option D: one database per tenant

:::decision{status=rejected}
Give each tenant its own database instance. This is the strongest isolation we could offer, and it is what the public health customer asked for.

For 1,840 tenants this is not affordable. The smallest database instance we can run with backups and a standby costs about 310 per month, which is more than the monthly price of our Team plan. Connection counts, migrations and monitoring would multiply by the number of tenants. Cross-tenant work that we do need, such as billing sync and product metrics, would have to read from 1,840 sources.

Outcome: rejected as the general model. Kept for the Dedicated plan only, where the customer pays for the instance. The Dedicated databases use the same schema and the same row-level security policies as the shared databases, so service code does not need a second code path.
:::

### Option E: separate clusters per customer size

:::decision{status=rejected}
Put small tenants on shared databases and move large tenants to a pool of medium-sized clusters, each holding 20 to 50 large tenants, all with application filtering. This came from the infrastructure team as a way to reduce noisy-neighbour problems at the same time.

It does help performance for the largest tenants, but it does not change isolation at all, since the control is still TenantScope. It would also need tenant moves between clusters, which we have never done without downtime.

Outcome: rejected as an isolation option. The noisy-neighbour work it was meant to address is tracked separately in RFC-0045.
:::

### Comparison

| Option | Isolation control | Effort (engineer-weeks) | Per-tenant cost | Migration risk | Outcome |
|-|-|-|-|-|-|
| A. App filtering plus checks | Application | 6 | none | low | Rejected |
| B. Row-level security | Database | 30 | none | medium | Accepted |
| C. Schema per tenant | Database | 45 | low | high | Rejected |
| D. Database per tenant | Infrastructure | 80+ | high | high | Rejected, kept for Dedicated |
| E. Clusters by size | Application | 25 | medium | high | Rejected |

## Decision

:::decision{status=accepted date=2026-09-22}
Option B is accepted, as decided at the architecture review on 2026-09-22. This RFC supersedes ADR-0007. From the end of phase 3 of the migration, isolation is enforced by Postgres row-level security on every tenant table, and TenantScope is a convenience layer, not the control. Service code must set the tenant through `with_tenant` at the start of each transaction, and services must use row-level security, not avoid it.

Response data gets per-tenant data encryption keys. Customers on the Dedicated plan get their own database instance with the same schema and policies. No other customer gets a dedicated database, whatever their size, unless they move to the Dedicated plan.
:::

ADR-0007 will be marked superseded in the ADR index when phase 3 finishes. Until then the index still shows it as accepted, because it is still how most of the code works, and teams should read this RFC for anything new.

Dissent was recorded from the reports team, who would have preferred to keep raw SQL in reports and add policies later. Their concern about report query plans is covered in Performance, and the reports team owns the benchmark for the six slowest report queries.

## Detailed design

### Tenant context in the database

Each tenant table gets one policy, created by a migration helper so that every policy has the same shape. The policy compares the row's `tenant_id` with the `app.tenant_id` setting. When the setting is missing, `current_setting` returns null, the comparison is never true, and the transaction sees no rows.

```sql
ALTER TABLE responses ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE responses FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON responses
  USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id', true)::uuid)
  WITH CHECK (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id', true)::uuid);

CREATE FUNCTION with_tenant(t uuid) RETURNS void
  LANGUAGE sql SECURITY DEFINER AS $$
  SELECT set_config('app.tenant_id', t::text, true);
$$;
```

`FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` makes the policy apply to the table owner as well, so a migration role that runs ad hoc queries is also limited. `WITH CHECK` stops a transaction from inserting or updating a row into another tenant. The third argument of `set_config` is `true`, which makes the setting local to the transaction.

Runtime roles get `EXECUTE` on `with_tenant` and no other way to change `app.tenant_id`. The function is owned by a role that services cannot log in as.

The `tenant_id` column must be the first column of at least one index on every tenant table. Most tables already have this because of TenantScope. The 9 that do not get one in phase 2.

### Tenant context in services

Every service gets the tenant from one of two places: the authenticated request (the session token carries the tenant id, checked by the gateway) or the job payload. It is never taken from a URL path, a query parameter or a request body, even when the API has the tenant id in its path for readability. If the path and the token disagree, the request fails with `404`, not `403`, so that a caller cannot learn whether another tenant's survey exists.

The shared database package gets a new entry point, `db.InTenant(ctx, fn)`, which opens a transaction, calls `with_tenant` with the tenant from the context, runs `fn`, and commits. The old `db.Begin` is kept for global tables and is renamed `db.BeginGlobal` so that its use stands out in review.

```go
err := db.InTenant(ctx, func(tx *db.Tx) error {
    rows, err := tx.Query(ctx,
        `SELECT id, answered_at FROM responses WHERE survey_id = $1`, surveyID)
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    defer rows.Close()
    return scanResponses(rows, &out)
})
```

Internal calls between services pass the tenant in a signed header that the receiving service checks against the caller's service identity. A service may only pass on the tenant it received. It cannot pick a different tenant for an internal call, and the receiving service rejects a header whose signature does not match. Internal calls without a tenant header are allowed only for global endpoints, which are listed in each service's API definition.

The query above has no `tenant_id` predicate, and it does not need one, since the policy adds it. TenantScope will keep adding the predicate for code that already uses it. A query plan with the predicate written twice is the same as with it once.

A transaction opened with `db.InTenant` holds a connection from the pool until it ends. Transactions that wait on network calls hold connections for longer than they need, so `db.InTenant` callbacks must not call other services. The package enforces this in tests only, by failing a test whose callback makes an outgoing HTTP call.

### Connection pooling and timeouts

The pooler stays in transaction mode. `SET LOCAL` settings end with the transaction, so a connection handed to the next client carries no tenant.

Each service instance uses at most 40 connections from the pooler, and the pooler allows 600 server connections per region. The statement timeout for service roles stays at 15 seconds. Report queries get 60 seconds, set per transaction in the reports service, and export queries run on the replica with a 10-minute timeout.

An idle-in-transaction timeout of 30 seconds is set for all runtime roles. It already existed for most roles and is added for `exports` and `reports`, which did not have it.

### Admin tools and support access

Querent staff do not have standing access to tenant data. The `admin-api` service, used by customer success and support, can read tenant metadata (plans, users, workspace names, usage counts) from global tables and from a small set of tenant tables that hold no response data. It cannot read response data at all, except through break-glass access.

Break-glass access gives one named engineer read access to one tenant for at most 4 hours. It needs a ticket that names the tenant and the reason, approval from the security on-call, and, for Enterprise and Dedicated tenants, written consent from the customer's named contact. The engineer uses a separate role that can call `with_tenant` only for the tenant on the ticket. Every query is logged to the audit log with the ticket number.

:::danger
> **Important:** Never copy response data out of a break-glass session into a ticket, a chat message or a local file. If a customer needs data, it goes to them through a normal export from their own workspace. Response data pasted into a ticket in 2025 had to be removed from three systems and reported to the customer as a data handling incident.
:::

Write access through break-glass is not allowed. A fix that needs to change tenant data is done with a reviewed migration script that runs under `with_tenant` for the one tenant, approved by the tenant's account owner at Querent and by security.

### Background jobs and queues

Every job payload carries `tenant_id` as a top-level field, set by the code that enqueues the job from its own transaction context. The worker reads it before anything else and runs the job inside `db.InTenant`. A job without `tenant_id` is rejected and moved to the dead-letter queue with an alert, unless the job type is on the list of global jobs.

Global jobs are jobs that work across tenants by design, such as billing sync, the nightly warehouse copy and usage counters. There are 11 of them today. A global job that needs tenant data loops over tenants and opens one `db.InTenant` transaction per tenant. It never reads tenant tables with a global role. New global job types need approval from the platform team, recorded in the job registry.

A single job processes at most 10,000 rows per transaction. Jobs with more work split it into batches, each with its own transaction, so that no transaction runs long enough to hit the idle or statement timeouts.

Retries keep the original `tenant_id`. A retry must never pick the tenant up from anywhere else.

### Exports

Exports are the path where INC-311 happened, so they get the strictest rules. An export runs as a job for one tenant and one survey or one workspace. It reads from the regional replica inside `db.InTenant`, writes the file to the tenant's prefix in object storage, and gives the user a signed link that expires after 24 hours.

A single export holds at most 500,000 responses or 2 GB of file data, whichever comes first. Larger exports are split into parts, and the user gets a link per part. A tenant can run at most 5 exports at the same time. Further exports wait in the queue.

Scheduled exports, which send a file to an SFTP server or a webhook the customer configures, follow the same rules, and the destination is stored per tenant. Scheduled exports to an email address are not supported, and the existing ones were turned off on 2026-08-01 after a 90-day notice.

The export worker no longer has raw SQL. All export queries were moved into the reports query library in phase 1, where they run through `db.InTenant` and are covered by the isolation tests.

### Search

The search cluster today has one index per region, holding open-text answers and survey metadata for every tenant, with `tenant_id` as a field. The reports service adds a `tenant_id` filter to every search. This is the same application-level model as ADR-0007, and the search cluster has no equivalent of row-level security that we trust.

From phase 4, each tenant gets its own index alias, `t_<tenant_id>`, with a filter on `tenant_id` built into the alias. Services search through the alias only, and the search client in the shared package takes the alias from the tenant context and refuses any other index name. Service credentials for the search cluster can read aliases but not the underlying index.

Tenants with more than 5 million responses get their own physical index instead of a filtered alias, so that their searches do not scan the shared index. There are 9 such tenants today. The threshold is checked weekly and a tenant that crosses it is moved by a reindex job, which takes about 40 minutes for a tenant of 5 million responses.

Search results never include respondent email addresses or names, even when the survey asked for them. Those fields are not indexed.

### Caching

The reports service and the collect service both cache in the regional cache cluster. Today the cache keys are built in each service, and some of them do not include the tenant, which is part of what went wrong in INC-311.

From phase 2, all cache access goes through a shared cache client that prefixes every key with `t:<tenant_id>:` from the tenant context. A key without a tenant can only be written through a separate global client, which is used for feature flags and plan limits and nothing else. Cache entries for response data have a time to live of at most 10 minutes, and aggregated report numbers at most 1 hour.

Flushing one tenant's cache entries is done by key prefix. It is part of tenant deletion and part of the break-glass close-out.

### Object storage

Uploaded files (images and documents that respondents attach, and logos tenants upload for their surveys) and export files live in one bucket per region. Today the object key starts with the survey id. From phase 2 it starts with the tenant id, `tenants/<tenant_id>/...`, and service credentials are scoped by a bucket policy to the prefix of the tenant in the request, using a short-lived credential the storage gateway issues per request.

Signed links for downloads expire after 24 hours for exports and 15 minutes for respondent uploads shown in reports. Public links to survey files are not supported. Tenant logos are the exception, since they are copied to the public assets bucket when a survey is published, because respondents need to see them without signing in.

Existing objects are copied to the new key layout in phase 2 by a background job and the old keys are deleted 30 days after the copy is verified. The job moves about 2 million objects an hour, so the full move takes about four days per region.

### Encryption keys

Response data, meaning answer values, free text, and respondent uploads, is encrypted with a per-tenant data encryption key. The key is generated when the tenant is created, stored wrapped by the regional key in the key management service, and cached unwrapped in service memory for at most 5 minutes.

Answer values in the database are encrypted at the column level by the collect service before they are written, and decrypted by the services that read them. This means the database never sees the plain values, and a database backup on its own does not expose response data. It also means that the database cannot search or sort on answer values. Reports that need that use the search index, which holds the decrypted text under its own disk encryption.

Tenant keys rotate every 90 days. New data uses the new key, and old data is re-encrypted by a background job within 30 days of the rotation. A tenant can ask for an extra rotation at any time through support, and Dedicated tenants can bring their own key, held in their own key management account, which we access through a grant they can revoke.

When a tenant's key is destroyed, its response data can no longer be read anywhere, including in backups. We use this as the final step of tenant deletion.

Other tenant data, such as survey definitions, user records and settings, is not encrypted per tenant. It stays encrypted at rest with the regional key, as it is today.

### Analytics warehouse

The warehouse gets a nightly copy of most tenant tables. Answer values are copied in their encrypted form and are not readable in the warehouse. Aggregates that product metrics need, such as response counts per survey and completion rates, are computed before the copy.

Some Enterprise customers query their own data in the warehouse through the reporting API, about 1,200 queries a day in total. Those queries currently go through a view per tenant, created by the reporting API when a tenant turns the feature on.

Whether the warehouse should get row-level security of its own, or move Enterprise reporting to a separate per-tenant store, is still open. It is listed under Open questions.

### Data retention and deletion

Isolation includes making sure that data is gone when it should be. This section covers how long data is kept and what happens when a tenant leaves. The retention periods are the company-wide ones, and they apply to the Dedicated databases too.

### Retention periods

:::include{file=../_shared/data-retention.smd}
:::

### Tenant deletion

When a tenant's contract ends, the tenant is marked closed and its users can no longer sign in. Thirty days later, the purge job deletes the tenant's rows from every tenant table, one table at a time inside `db.InTenant`, removes its search alias or index, deletes its object storage prefix and flushes its cache prefix. The tenant's data encryption key is destroyed last, after the purge job has confirmed every step.

A closed tenant can be reopened by support during the 30 days, and its data comes back as it was. After the purge, nothing can be restored, and support has no way to recover data for a tenant that comes back later.

The purge runs table by table and does not lock the tenant tables for other tenants. For the largest tenant today it would take about 3 hours. It is limited to one tenant at a time per region, so that the purge never competes with itself for I/O.

Deletion of a Dedicated tenant also removes the database instance, after a final backup that is kept for 35 days like any other backup and then expires.

### Dedicated plan

A Dedicated tenant gets its own database instance in its home region, with its own standby and backups. Everything else, the services, the search cluster, object storage and the cache, is shared, with the isolation described above. The Dedicated database uses the same migrations, the same row-level security policies and the same roles as the shared database. Services find the right database through the tenant directory, which maps a tenant id to a connection string, and they cache that lookup for 60 seconds.

Customers on the Dedicated plan can choose a maintenance window for their database, in a 4-hour block once a month. Migrations for Dedicated databases run in that window, which means a Dedicated database can be up to one month behind the shared schema. Services must therefore handle the previous schema version for at least 35 days after a migration ships. This is a constraint on every schema change, not only for the Dedicated plan, and the migration review checklist has a line for it.

The Dedicated plan does not include a dedicated search index or dedicated object storage bucket. Customers who ask for those are told that their data in those systems is isolated by alias and prefix, and that the Dedicated plan covers the database only. Sales has a one-page summary of this.

A tenant can move from a shared database to the Dedicated plan, but not back. Moving to Dedicated is done by a logical copy of the tenant's rows, followed by a cut-over of a few minutes during which the tenant's surveys accept responses into a buffer. We have done this three times, and the longest cut-over was 6 minutes.

### Audit logging

Every access to tenant data that does not come from the tenant's own users is written to the audit log: break-glass sessions, global jobs, support actions in `admin-api`, and purge runs. Each entry has the tenant id, the actor, the ticket or job id, and the tables or objects touched. Audit entries are written to a separate append-only store, and no runtime role can delete them.

Enterprise and Dedicated tenants can download their own audit entries from the admin console, filtered to their tenant. Other tenants can ask for them through support. Audit entries are kept according to the retention periods above.

### Services that stay global

Some services work across tenants by design. They keep working that way, with tighter limits on what they can read.

`billing-sync` reads usage counts per tenant every hour and sends them to the billing system. From phase 3 it reads the counts from a global table, `tenant_usage`, which the services update inside their own tenant transactions. It no longer reads tenant tables at all. `tenant_usage` holds counts and dates only.

`notify` sends email invitations to respondents for every tenant. It reads the send list for one survey at a time inside `db.InTenant`, so it is a tenant service for reads, and it writes delivery events to a global queue that holds no addresses, only message ids. Invitations are sent from a per-tenant sending domain when the tenant has set one up, and otherwise from the shared Querent domain.

Feature flags, plan limits and the tenant directory are global tables read by every service. They hold no response data and they change rarely, so services cache them for 60 seconds through the global cache client.

The product metrics pipeline reads from the warehouse, not from the primary database, and only sees aggregates and encrypted answer values, as described in Analytics warehouse.

### Local development

Developers run the services locally against a Postgres container with the same migrations and policies as production. The seed script creates three tenants, two small ones and one with 200,000 synthetic responses, so that developers see the effect of the policies while they work.

```bash
make db-up            # starts Postgres with migrations and policies
make seed TENANTS=3   # synthetic tenants, never production data
make test-isolation   # runs the isolation suite against local services
```

The local database role used by services has the same rights as in production, so a query outside `db.InTenant` returns no rows locally too. Developers who need to look at all tenants' rows locally use the migration role with `psql`, which is fine on synthetic data. The migration role's password is not shared between developers and production, and the production migration role is never used from a laptop.

### Public API behaviour

The public API does not change shape. Its behaviour changes in three small ways that customers could notice.

First, as described above, a request for an object in another tenant returns `404`. Before this work some endpoints returned `403` in that case, mostly in the reports API. The API changelog will list them.

Second, rate limits become per tenant as well as per API key. Each tenant can make at most 300 requests a second across all its keys, and each key at most 50. The per-key limit is unchanged. The per-tenant limit is new and protects other tenants on the same database from one tenant's scripts. Dedicated tenants have no per-tenant limit, since they do not share a database, but the per-key limit still applies to them.

Third, bulk endpoints that accept a list of ids (for example, fetch up to 100 responses by id) now return only the ids that belong to the caller's tenant, without an error for the others. Before, they failed the whole request if any id was unknown. This is the same "not yours looks like missing" rule as the 404.

Webhooks are sent per tenant from a queue partitioned by tenant, so a slow webhook endpoint for one tenant cannot delay another tenant's deliveries. A tenant can have at most 20 webhook endpoints. Webhook payloads include the tenant id so that customers with several Querent tenants can tell them apart.

## Migration plan

The migration runs in five phases. No phase needs downtime, and each one can be rolled back on its own until phase 5. Each service team owns the work in its own service, and the platform team owns the shared packages, the policies and the schedule.

| Phase | What changes | Owner | Target finish | Rollback |
|-|-|-|-|-|
| 1. Prepare | `db.InTenant` package, linter without silence comment, export queries moved to the reports library | Platform, reports | 2026-10-16 | Revert package version |
| 2. Keys and paths | Tenant index on 9 tables, tenant-prefixed cache keys, object storage layout, per-tenant encryption keys for new data | Platform, collect | 2026-11-20 | Feature flags per service |
| 3. Policies on | RLS policies created and forced on all 87 tenant tables, services switched to `db.InTenant` | All service teams | 2027-01-29 | Disable policies per table |
| 4. Search | Per-tenant aliases, large-tenant indexes, search client change | Reports | 2027-02-26 | Point client back to regional index |
| 5. Clean up | TenantScope reduced to a helper, old cache keys and object keys removed, ADR-0007 marked superseded | Platform | 2027-03-31 | None |

```mermaid
gantt
  accTitle: Tenant isolation migration phases
  dateFormat YYYY-MM-DD
  section Phases
  1 Prepare            :done, p1, 2026-09-28, 2026-10-16
  2 Keys and paths     :active, p2, 2026-10-19, 2026-11-20
  3 Policies on        :p3, 2026-11-23, 2027-01-29
  4 Search             :p4, 2027-02-01, 2027-02-26
  5 Clean up           :p5, 2027-03-01, 2027-03-31
```

### Phase 3 in detail

Phase 3 is where the risk is, so it gets more detail here.

Policies are turned on table by table, in an order the platform team keeps in the migration tracker. Small global-looking tables go first, then the tables each service owns, and the `responses` and `answers` tables last. A table's policy is created disabled, then enabled in shadow mode, then enforced.

In shadow mode, the policy is not enforced. Instead, a trigger-free check in the database package compares the result of every query on that table with and without the tenant filter on a 1% sample, and logs any query that would have returned rows from more than one tenant. A table stays in shadow mode for at least 7 days and until the shadow log shows no cross-tenant results for 72 hours. Then the policy is forced.

Services move to `db.InTenant` before the tables they use are forced. A service that still has a code path without `db.InTenant` when its tables are forced will see empty results on that path. That is the intended failure mode. It is safer than leaking rows, and the shadow log should have found such paths first.

The order of work for each table is:

1. Create the policy disabled, and the tenant index if missing.
2. Turn on shadow mode for the table.
3. Wait at least 7 days, with no cross-tenant results in the last 72 hours.
4. Enable and force the policy in the EU region.
5. Wait 48 hours, then do the same in the US region.

Forcing happens during working hours in the EU, Tuesday to Thursday, never on a Friday or before a public holiday in either region. The engineer who forces a table watches the error rates and the `db_tx_without_tenant_total` metric for that table for an hour afterwards, and the owning service team is told a day ahead so that someone from the team is around.

Rollback for a forced table is to disable its policy, which takes effect at once and does not need a deploy. The on-call platform engineer can do this without approval during an incident. Outside an incident it needs the platform lead.

### Dedicated databases during the migration

The three Dedicated databases get each phase one week after the shared database in their region, in their maintenance window. This gives a week to find problems on the shared database first, where we have far more traffic to find them with.

### Freeze

There is no code freeze for the migration. There is a schema freeze on the `responses` and `answers` tables from the start of phase 3 until both regions have their policies forced on those tables. Changes to those two tables during the freeze need approval from the platform lead.

## Testing and verification

Isolation is only as good as the evidence that it works, and enterprise customers will ask for that evidence. Testing has four parts.

**Isolation test suite.** A shared test harness creates two tenants, A and B, with the same surveys, users and responses, and then runs every service's API tests as tenant A while checking that no response, log line, cache entry, search hit or exported file contains any of tenant B's marker values. Each tenant's data carries unique marker strings in free text, file names and survey titles, so a leak shows up as a string match. The suite runs on every merge to main for all seven services and takes about 11 minutes. It is the test that blocks deploys, as the Requirements section says.

**Policy coverage check.** A CI job reads the schema after migrations and fails if any tenant table has no policy, a policy that does not use `app.tenant_id`, or row-level security not forced. It also fails if any runtime role has `BYPASSRLS` or owns a tenant table. From phase 3, a new tenant table cannot be merged without a policy.

**Fuzzing the tenant context.** A nightly job replays a sample of 50,000 real API requests from the previous day against a staging copy, with the tenant in the session token swapped for a random other tenant. Every request must return `404` or an empty result. A request that returns data is a failed check and opens a security ticket automatically.

**External test.** Before we describe this isolation to customers as complete, an external security firm tests it, with access to two test tenants and the source code. The test is booked for March 2027, after phase 4. Findings rated high or critical must be fixed before phase 5 ends.

What we do not test: we do not run isolation tests against production tenant data, and we do not create test tenants in production except for the two synthetic tenants that monitoring uses. Production tests that need real data volume use the staging copy, which holds synthetic responses generated at the same scale.

### Isolation tests in pull requests

The full isolation suite runs on merge to main. Pull requests run a smaller version that covers only the endpoints the change touches, chosen from the service's API definition, and takes about 2 minutes. A pull request that adds a new endpoint must add it to the isolation suite in the same pull request, and the CI check fails if a new endpoint has no isolation test. Reviewers are asked to look at the isolation test for a new endpoint as closely as at the endpoint itself, since a test that never logs in as the second tenant proves nothing.

### Test data

The staging copy holds 1,840 synthetic tenants with the same size distribution as production, generated by the data team's synthetic data tool. It is rebuilt every month. It never holds a copy of production response data, even pseudonymised, and requests to load real customer data into staging are refused.

## Performance

The concern with row-level security, raised in ADR-0007 and again by the reports team, is that the policy predicate changes query plans on large tables. We measured this in June 2026 on a copy of the EU database with the policies forced, replaying a day of production queries.

| Query group | p95 before | p95 with RLS | Change |
|-|-|-|-|
| Collect writes (insert answer) | 6.1 ms | 6.3 ms | +3% |
| Builder reads (survey definition) | 4.4 ms | 4.5 ms | +2% |
| Report summary (one survey) | 210 ms | 219 ms | +4% |
| Report cross-tab (one survey, 5 questions) | 1.9 s | 2.6 s | +37% |
| Export page (5,000 rows) | 340 ms | 352 ms | +4% |

Everything except the cross-tab report was within the 5% budget. The cross-tab report was slower because the planner chose a sequential scan on `answers` once the policy predicate was added, since the predicate and the join condition used different indexes. Adding a composite index on `answers (tenant_id, survey_id, question_id)` brought it back to 1.95 s, within budget. The index is part of phase 2.

The p95 latency budget for the main read paths is 5% over the June baseline, measured per query group over a week. A group that goes over budget after its tables are forced has 2 weeks to get back under, after which the platform team can roll back the policies on those tables and the service team owns the fix.

The six slowest report queries are benchmarked again before the `answers` table is forced, by the reports team. If any of them is more than 20% slower, the forcing of `answers` waits until it is fixed.

Database CPU went up by about 2% with all policies forced. That fits in the current headroom of the primary databases, which run at about 45% CPU at peak.

## Monitoring

New metrics and alerts, all per region:

- `rls_shadow_cross_tenant_total`, the count of shadow-mode queries that would have returned rows from more than one tenant, per table. Any non-zero value during phase 3 pages the platform on-call during working hours.
- `db_tx_without_tenant_total`, transactions on tenant tables that ran without `app.tenant_id`, per service. After phase 3 this should be zero, and a non-zero value raises a ticket for the service team.
- `job_missing_tenant_total`, jobs rejected for a missing `tenant_id`. Alerts at more than 10 in an hour.
- `breakglass_sessions_active`, the number of open break-glass sessions. Any session older than 4 hours pages security.
- The isolation test suite result per service, on the deploy dashboard.

Two synthetic tenants exist in production for monitoring, one in each region. A canary job signs in as each of them every 5 minutes and tries to read the other synthetic tenant's survey by id, through the API, the search endpoint and the export endpoint. Any answer other than a 404 or an empty result pages the security on-call at once, day or night. These are the only tenants in production created for testing.

Dashboards for the migration are on the platform team's board, under "Tenant isolation". They show each table's state (no policy, shadow, forced), shadow-mode findings, and latency per query group against the budget.

## Customer communication

Customers are not told about the migration while it runs, except for Dedicated customers, whose maintenance windows are used. There is no change in the product that customers can see.

When phase 4 is finished and the external test is passed, the security team publishes an updated security whitepaper with a section on tenant isolation, and the sales team gets an updated answer for the isolation questions in security questionnaires. Until then, sales answers those questions with the current text, which describes application-level isolation, and must not describe row-level security as in place. The legal team has asked for this so that we do not promise controls that are not finished.

Customers who were affected by INC-311 got a direct update from their account owner in October 2026 describing this work and its timeline, as promised in the incident report.

## Evidence for customer reviews

Security reviews by enterprise customers ask the same questions again and again. The security team keeps the full answers, and this table shows which part of the design answers which question, so that engineers know what evidence they will be asked to produce.

| Customer question | Control in this RFC | Evidence |
|-|-|-|
| How is our data separated from other customers' data? | Row-level security on every tenant table | Policy coverage check output, schema export |
| Can your staff read our survey responses? | Break-glass access only, with consent for Enterprise and Dedicated | Audit log entries for the tenant, break-glass procedure |
| Is our data encrypted with keys specific to us? | Per-tenant keys for response data | Key management audit trail, rotation log |
| How do you test that isolation works? | Isolation suite, fuzzing, external test | Latest suite result, fuzzing summary, external test letter |
| What happens to our data when we leave? | Retention periods and tenant deletion | Purge job record for the tenant, key destruction record |
| Can we have a separate database? | Dedicated plan | Plan description |

Engineers are asked for evidence through the security team, never directly by the customer. If a customer contact asks an engineer for a schema export or a log, the engineer sends the request to the security team and does not answer it themselves. The security team checks that the evidence holds no other tenant's data before it goes out.

The external test letter will be available from April 2027. Until then, the security team answers the testing question with the internal suite results only.

## Risks

:::risk{impact=medium likelihood=medium}
Risk: a code path that does not use `db.InTenant` is missed by shadow mode, because it runs rarely (for example a yearly report or an admin action), and returns empty results after its tables are forced. Impact medium, likelihood medium. Mitigation: the `db_tx_without_tenant_total` metric shows such paths in staging and production, and an empty result is a visible bug rather than a leak.
:::

:::risk{impact=high likelihood=low}
Risk: query plans on `answers` change after forcing in a way the June benchmark did not catch, and report latency goes over budget for large tenants. Impact high, likelihood low. Mitigation: the six slowest report queries are benchmarked again before forcing, and policies can be turned off per table in seconds.
:::

:::risk{impact=medium likelihood=high}
Risk: the 140 places with a silenced linter comment hide more raw SQL than we think, and phase 3 takes longer than planned. Impact medium, likelihood high. Mitigation: phase 1 counts and assigns every one of them. The migration plan has four weeks of slack in phase 3 for this.
:::

:::risk{impact=high likelihood=low}
Risk: per-tenant keys add a dependency on the key management service to every read of response data. Impact high, likelihood low. Mitigation: unwrapped keys are cached for 5 minutes, and the key management service has its own regional redundancy. An outage longer than 5 minutes would stop reports, exports and response collection until it ends.
:::

## Operations

### On-call changes

The platform on-call runbook gets three new procedures. They are summarised here, and the runbook has the exact steps.

**Turning off a policy on one table.** Used when a forced table causes an outage, for example a service returning empty results on a hot path. The on-call engineer runs the disable statement through the admin console, which records who ran it and why, and posts in the incident channel. Turning off a policy does not leak data by itself, since services still filter through TenantScope where they did before, but it removes the guarantee, so the policy must be turned back on within 24 hours or the security on-call must be told.

```sql
-- run through the admin console, never from a laptop
ALTER TABLE answers NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE answers DISABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
-- to restore
ALTER TABLE answers ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
ALTER TABLE answers FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
```

**A suspected cross-tenant leak.** Any report or alert that suggests one tenant saw another tenant's data is a security incident from the start, at severity 2 or higher, and the security on-call leads it. The platform on-call's job is to find the code path and stop it, by turning off the feature flag, rolling back the deploy or blocking the endpoint at the gateway. Turning off row-level security is never the fix for a leak.

**Break-glass requests out of hours.** Break-glass requests outside working hours need the security on-call to approve them, as during the day. For Enterprise and Dedicated tenants, the customer's consent is still needed, so an out-of-hours request for those tenants usually waits until the customer answers. There is no emergency path around consent.

### Capacity

The changes add some load. Shadow mode doubles the reads for the 1% sample of queries on tables in shadow, which is about 0.5% extra database load while a large table is in shadow. Per-tenant search aliases add no load. Per-tenant indexes for large tenants add about 8% more disk to the search cluster, which is within its current headroom. The storage gateway issues about 1,500 short-lived credentials a second at peak, and is sized for 5,000.

The key management service is called when a key is not in a service's cache, which with 5-minute caching means about 2,000 calls a minute per region at peak. Its quota for our account is 10,000 calls a minute per region. If we come close to that, the cache time can go up to 15 minutes, but not higher, and the security team must agree to the change.

## Service checklist

Each service team works through this list before the platform team forces the policies on the tables that service owns. The platform team tracks it per service in the migration tracker. The list is the same for every service, and the owner and date shown are for the phase 3 kick-off items that are not tied to one service.

- [x] Every database call on a tenant table goes through `db.InTenant`, or through `db.BeginGlobal` on a global table.
- [x] Every job the service enqueues carries `tenant_id` at the top level.
- [ ] No cache key is built outside the shared cache client.
- [ ] No object storage key is built outside the storage gateway.
- [ ] The service's API tests pass in the isolation suite with two tenants.
- [ ] The service's `db_tx_without_tenant_total` has been zero in staging for 7 days.
- [ ] Publish the table order for phase 3 in the migration tracker, :priority[P1], @dana, due :due[2026-11-16]
- [ ] Book the external isolation test for March 2027, :priority[P2], @kofi, due :due[2026-12-15]
- [ ] Re-run the six slowest report benchmarks on the phase 2 indexes, :priority[P1], @rafael, due :due[2026-11-27]

The first two items are already ticked because they were finished for every service in phase 1. They stay in the list so that new services go through them.

## Work per team

Rough estimates, agreed with each team lead in September 2026. They add up to about 31 engineer-weeks, close to the 30 estimated at the July review.

| Team | Main work | Estimate (engineer-weeks) |
|-|-|-|
| Platform | `db.InTenant`, policies, migration helper, shadow mode, CI checks, cache client, storage gateway | 11 |
| Collect | Column encryption of answers, job payloads, cache keys | 5 |
| Reports | Raw SQL moved to the query library, search client, report benchmarks | 7 |
| Exports | Export worker on `db.InTenant`, part splitting, scheduled export destinations | 3 |
| Builder | Move to `db.InTenant` | 1.5 |
| Admin and support tools | `admin-api` limits, break-glass role and audit | 2.5 |
| Data | Warehouse copy changes, synthetic staging data | 1 |

`notify` and `billing-sync` need less than a week each and are covered by the platform team. The security team's time for reviews and the external test is not in the table.

## Frequently asked questions

**Can I still write raw SQL?** Yes, inside `db.InTenant`. The policy filters raw SQL the same way as anything else. What you cannot do any more is read tenant tables outside a tenant transaction, because you will get no rows.

**What about migrations that touch every tenant's rows?** Migrations run as the migration role, which owns the tables, and the policies are forced, so the migration role also sees nothing without a tenant. Data migrations that change rows loop over tenants with `with_tenant`, like global jobs do. Schema migrations (adding a column or an index) are not affected by policies at all.

**Does `with_tenant` work with read replicas?** Yes. The setting is per transaction on whichever server runs it, and the policies replicate with the schema.

**What happens to a request with no tenant, like a health check or a public survey page?** Health checks do not touch tenant tables. Public survey pages are served by `collect`, which looks up the survey by its public link in a global table, `survey_links`, that maps the link to a tenant and survey id. It then opens a tenant transaction for that tenant. `survey_links` holds no response data, which is why it is allowed to be global.

**Can a respondent's answers move to another tenant if a survey is transferred?** No. Survey transfer between tenants is not supported and was never supported. A tenant that wants to give a survey to another tenant exports the definition and the other tenant imports it, without responses.

**Do the policies apply to the Dedicated databases?** Yes, the same policies. On a Dedicated database every row belongs to one tenant, so the policy never filters anything out. We keep it so that the code path is the same and so that a wrong tenant id still returns nothing.

**Why 404 and not 403 when the tenant does not match?** A 403 tells the caller that the object exists in another tenant. A 404 does not. The public API documentation already says that a `404` can mean "not yours".

**Is TenantScope going away?** Not entirely. It stays as a small helper that adds the predicate, which some teams like for readability and which helps the planner in a few places. It is no longer the control, and the review checklist no longer treats a missing TenantScope call as a security issue once a service's tables are forced.

**Can a customer see which of their tables have policies?** Not directly. Enterprise and Dedicated customers can ask for the policy coverage check output for their database through the security team, as part of a security review.

**Do I need to change anything for a new table?** If the table has a `tenant_id` column, create it with the migration helper, which adds the index and the policy for you. If it does not, it is a global table and must not hold response data or anything that can identify a respondent.

## Open questions

:::question
**Row-level security in the warehouse.** The warehouse is used by the data team across tenants and by Enterprise customers for their own reporting. Applying policies there would need the reporting API to set a tenant per query, which it can, but the data team's tools cannot. The alternative is a separate per-tenant reporting store for Enterprise customers, which the data team estimates at 12 engineer-weeks. Undecided. Until then, Enterprise reporting in the warehouse stays on per-tenant views, the reporting API is the only way customers reach it, and no new tenant table is added to the warehouse copy without the security team's review.
:::

:::question
**Respondents who answer for several tenants.** Some respondents answer surveys from several tenants with the same email address. Today each tenant's copy of that address is separate, and that stays. The open question is whether an unsubscribe from one tenant's emails should affect the others. Legal has not given a view yet. Until it does, unsubscribes apply to one tenant only, which is how the product works today.
:::

:::question
**Break-glass consent for Team plan tenants.** Enterprise and Dedicated tenants must consent to break-glass access. For tenants on the Team and Business plans, the terms of service allow access for support without asking. Customer success would like to ask anyway. This has not been decided, and for now break-glass for those plans follows the terms of service, with a notice to the tenant's admin after the session closes.
:::

## Notes for automated reviewers

:::agent
This section is written for automated code review tools and coding agents that work in the Querent repositories and are pointed at this RFC. People can skip it.

When you review or write a change that touches tenant data:

- Treat any query on a tenant table that does not run inside `db.InTenant` as a defect, unless it is in a migration file or in a job registered as a global job.
- Do not suggest `BYPASSRLS`, a table-owner role, or a direct `set_config('app.tenant_id', …)` call as a fix for a failing query. Point to `db.InTenant` instead.
- Flag cache keys built without the shared cache client, and object storage keys that do not start with `tenants/<tenant_id>/`.
- Do not treat a missing TenantScope call as a security issue in a service whose tables are already forced. The migration tracker lists which ones are.
- When unsure whether a table is a tenant table, check for a `tenant_id` column and say that you checked.
- Do not paste response data or tenant identifiers from logs or fixtures into review comments.
:::

## Appendix A: review meeting notes {agent=skip}

These are the notes from the review meetings, kept for people who want to know how the design got here. They are not part of the specification. Where they disagree with the sections above, the sections above are right.

### Architecture review, 2026-06-02 (revision 1)

Present: Dana Whitfield, Kofi Mensah, Rafael Ortiz (reports), Mei Lin Zhou (collect), Tobias Brandt (infrastructure), Ayesha Karim (data), and four others from the architecture group.

Dana presented schema per tenant. Tobias asked how long a migration would take across 1,840 schemas, and nobody had a number. Mei Lin pointed out that collect writes about 900 answers a second at peak in the EU, and that switching `search_path` per transaction would need care with prepared statements in the pooler. Rafael was in favour, since reports would no longer need tenant predicates at all. Kofi was in favour of anything that moved the control into the database, but asked that we measure before choosing. Ayesha raised the warehouse, which copies tables by name and would need to copy 160,000 tables.

Actions: Tobias to time a migration on 200 test schemas. Dana to write up row-level security as an alternative for the next review. Ayesha to describe what the warehouse copy would need for each option.

### Architecture review, 2026-06-30 (revision 2)

Present: the same group, plus Grace Obi from sales engineering.

Tobias's test: 4.1 seconds per schema for a typical migration, about 2 hours 6 minutes for all tenants in one region, with one failure out of 200 that left a schema half migrated. That ended the all-tenants variant. Dana presented revision 2, schemas for Enterprise tenants only. Grace said the three prospects would accept either schemas or row-level security as long as there was test evidence, and that one of them needed a separate database regardless. Kofi said two isolation models would double the testing and that he would rather have one good model for everyone. Rafael changed his view after seeing the June benchmark numbers for row-level security, apart from the cross-tab query.

The meeting agreed to write revision 3 around row-level security, with a dedicated database as a paid plan. No decision was taken.

### Architecture review, 2026-07-14 (revision 3)

Present: the same group.

Revision 3 was reviewed. Options A, C and E were rejected in the meeting, as recorded in Options considered. Option D was rejected as the general model and kept for the paid plan. Option B was agreed in principle, pending the cross-tab index fix and a cost estimate from each team. Rafael recorded the reports team's dissent about moving raw SQL before the policies, and asked that the report benchmarks be a gate for forcing `answers`, which was agreed.

There was a long discussion about whether to encrypt all tenant data with per-tenant keys. Ayesha pointed out it would make the warehouse useless for product metrics, and Mei Lin estimated that encrypting survey definitions as well would add 2 to 3 milliseconds to every builder read. The group settled on response data only.

### Security review, 2026-08-19

Present: Kofi Mensah, Dana Whitfield, two engineers from the security team, and the data protection officer.

The security team went through revision 3 line by line. They asked for three changes, all made in revision 4. Break-glass access for Enterprise and Dedicated tenants needs the customer's written consent. Tenant mismatches return 404 everywhere, including the reports API. The canary between the two synthetic production tenants pages at any hour, not only in working hours. The data protection officer asked whether per-tenant keys meant we could promise customers that deleted data is unreadable in backups, and Kofi said yes, for response data only, once the key is destroyed. The sales one-pager says exactly that and no more.

There was a question about whether the warehouse should be in scope. The group agreed to leave it as an open question rather than delay the RFC.

### Architecture review, 2026-09-22 (revision 4)

Present: Dana Whitfield, Kofi Mensah, Rafael Ortiz, Mei Lin Zhou, Tobias Brandt, Ayesha Karim, and the head of engineering.

Revision 4 added the cost estimates from each team, the cross-tab index fix, the break-glass consent rule for Enterprise and Dedicated tenants, and the 404 rule for tenant mismatches. The head of engineering asked whether 30 engineer-weeks was realistic given the linter silences, and Dana said the phase 3 slack was there for that. The RFC was accepted. Phase 1 started on 2026-09-28.

## Appendix B: revision history {agent=skip}

- **Revision 1, 2026-05-21.** First draft. Proposed one Postgres schema per tenant for all tenants, with schema migrations run by a new orchestrator.
- **Revision 2, 2026-06-23.** Schemas for Enterprise tenants only, application filtering for the rest. Added the June row-level security benchmark as an appendix.
- **Revision 3, 2026-07-08.** Row-level security for all tenants, Dedicated plan for separate databases, per-tenant keys for response data. Moved the benchmark into Performance.
- **Revision 4, 2026-09-15.** Team estimates, cross-tab index, break-glass consent, 404 rule, warehouse question moved to Open questions. Accepted on 2026-09-22.
- **Revision 4.1, 2026-09-30.** Corrected the export limits (they had been given as 1 million responses in revision 4) and added the FAQ.

## Appendix C: INC-311 in brief {agent=skip}

This is a short account of the incident for readers who were not here at the time. The full incident report is in the incident archive.

On 2025-11-12 at 09:40, a customer success manager ran a scheduled export for a market research tenant, as a favour, because the customer's own scheduled export had failed overnight. The manager opened the file to check it before sending it and noticed survey titles that did not belong to the customer. She stopped, did not send the file, and raised it in the support channel at 09:58. The security on-call opened an incident at 10:05.

The export worker had a cross-tab query, added in March 2025, that joined responses to surveys through a cache of survey ids. The cache key was the survey's short code, which is unique only inside a tenant. Two tenants had surveys with the same short code, and the cached list for one tenant's export included the other tenant's survey. The query filtered surveys by tenant correctly, but responses were joined through the cached ids, so 212 responses from the second tenant came along.

By 13:20 the export worker had a fix deployed that keyed the cache by tenant and short code. By the end of the next day, every export from the previous 90 days had been replayed against the fixed code and compared with what was sent. No other export had included another tenant's data. The second tenant was told on 2025-11-13, within 48 hours of the incident starting, and their response data had not left Querent, since the file was never sent.

The incident review found three causes: raw SQL outside TenantScope, a cache key without the tenant, and a review process that relied on reviewers reading SQL carefully in long files. It also noted that the linter had been silenced on the query with a comment that said "tenant filtered below". The review recommended database-level isolation, which became this RFC.
